$$ Reopen a dump written by catch_failfast with the same symbols the live session
$$ had. The point of keeping the dump at all is the question we did not think to
$$ ask before the process died, and that has happened twice now.

.symfix
.sympath+ F:\rbo_fabre_proto\d3d9_sw\symbols
.sympath+ SRV*F:\rbo_fabre_proto\d3d9_sw\symbols-cache*https://symbolserver.unity3d.com/
.reload /f

.echo === what killed it ===
.exr -1
.echo
.echo === faulting thread, with frame pointers and inlines ===
kv
.echo
.echo === the fail-fast subcode lives in rcx ===
r rcx
.echo
.echo === every thread, briefly ===
~*k
.echo
.echo === modules, to confirm which symbols actually resolved ===
lm
.echo
.echo === automatic triage, read sceptically ===
!analyze -v

$$ Left interactive rather than quitting: the dump exists precisely so follow-up
$$ questions can be asked, and the useful ones are never known in advance. Try
$$ !list on the corrupt list head, dt _LIST_ENTRY on the entries either side, and
$$ !address on any pointer whose provenance is in doubt.
